  /*
    Actually there's a digression I shouldn't delay any longer.
    All our demonstrations are valid because the factor H (s) is not a function of time.
    
    We used its symbol to solve the equations -- its formula could have been anything.
    As long as it remains time-invariant and a factor (or a sum of factors), our demonstrations can be generalised.

    A function only defined by additions and/or multiplications is called "linear".
    (Subtractions are additions. Divisions are multiplications)

    So we've shown that any linear time-invariant circuit doesn't generate partials (apart from contingent DC offset)
    nor does it shift the frequency of extant ones -- which, inside a spectral integral, is equivalent to generating new partial(s).
    It only affects their complex amplitude (real amplitude and phase).
    I didn't just coin the term "linear time-invariant": it's usually abbreviated "LTI" and is defined by what we've been demonstrating.
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_time-invariant_system

    Notice that if the cutoff frequency (ωc, fc) or any parameter varies over time, LTI no longer applies.
